  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 218 AM CST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nA large scale, slow moving upper level low remains anchored over the \nnortheast U.S., with cyclonic, generally northerly flow on the \nwestern periphery of this low continuing over central IL. Embedded \nin this northerly flow around the upper low are a few shortwave \nfeatures serving to provide periods of enhanced lift and shift the \nflow direction in the vicinity of Lake Michigan, allowing Lake-\nEffect shower bands to aim at portions of east-central IL. It is \nanticipated that these showers could affect this area through \nthis evening. Temperatures will be marginal for snow and much of\nthis activity seems likely to dissipate by reaching east-\ncentral IL, but some snowflakes and/or graupel could \noccasionally be noticeable, and some minor accumulations on \ngrassy/elevated surfaces can't be ruled out. Friday night \nthrough the weekend the northeast upper low looks to change \norientation and lift northeastward as a ridge over the west \nshifts east, ending the orientation of flow from Lake Michigan \nto east-central IL and producing a subsident, dry air mass over \ncentral IL that should remain well into next week. The next \nchance for precipitation looks to arrive late next week as the \ncentral U.S. ridge shifts far enough east that a cold front \ntrailing a shortwave/surface low in the upper Midwest could \nbring some minor precipitation to central IL. \n\nTemperatures will run below seasonal normals the next few days in \nnortherly flow, with highs in the lower to middle 40s today, upper \n40s Saturday, and around 50 Sunday. The warming trend will continue \ninto next week, with highs above normal by midweek (upper 50s to \nlower 60s Wednesday/Thursday). Lows in the 20s can be expected the \nnext several nights, a bit below normal, but not unusual for mid \nNovember.",
